  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMostly dry conditions are expected to continue through tonight as \nhigh pressure sits overhead this afternoon. The high will then begin \nshifting to the northeast tonight as it gives way to multiple low \npressures that will move across Northern New England this weekend. \n\nFor tonight, other than the coast and southern NH, skies should stay \nmainly clear, allowing temperatures to fall into the 50s \nsouth of the mountains with some 40s to the north. More valley fog \nis likely along the CT River, and I do have some low PoPs (20%) in \nsoutheast NH as some models indicate a few showers are possible \novernight.\n\nThe first low then approaches from the south Saturday morning with \nskies becoming mostly cloudy, generally in a south to north \nprogression through the day as the low carries higher moisture into \nthe area. It will also bring scattered showers (with dry periods as \nwell), although models have been struggling on a run to run basis \nregarding timing, coverage, and location of showers. Looking at \nlatest guidance, global models indicate broad lift over the Gulf of \nMaine approaching the coast and southeast NH in the morning and \nearly afternoon, which will the primary areas to watch for showers. \nLift spreads northward through the afternoon and evening \n(mostly across western ME), bringing increasing chances for \nshowers, and the 12Z NAMNest and HRRR are generally on board \nwith this scenario. Rainfall accumulations are expected to \nremain light, but parts of western ME could pick up over 0.25\" \nof rain through Saturday evening.\n\nMore uncertainty exists Saturday night into Sunday morning. Some \nmodels favor a lull in the showers due to some mid-level \ndrying while the low-levels remain nearly saturated, suggesting fog \n(possibly dense) and precipitation that falls more in the form of \ndrizzle. Others indicate another wave of low pressure, which would \nbring more in the way of showers and less in the way of fog. Due to \nthe uncertainty, PoPs of 20-40% continue to be advertised.\n\nSunday remains cloudy and dreary for most with scattered showers \npossible at times in the morning. Another low pressures tracks \nacross Quebec that will send a cold front in from the west, \nresulting in another increase in shower chances and possibly a storm \nor two in the afternoon and evening hours. Some showers continue \ninto Sunday night, but these should diminish south of the mountains \nfrom west to east overnight behind the front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nClosed low moving east of the Great Lakes will continue to \npivot into southern Quebec Monday. Showers in the morning exit \nnortheast across southern ME, with thinning clouds across NH. An\nembedded wave in the low's circulation will lift north through \nthe morning, with building instability through mid afternoon. \nThis could result in a rejuvenated shower/thunderstorm chance.\n\nThe thunderstorm parameter space has some ingredients going for\nit, but also limitations. 0-6km shear of around 50 kts is \nabundant on the periphery of the broad low, and this overlaps \nwith marginal surface CAPE early Monday. Low and mid level lapse\nrates would both be supportive of convection, however its \npossible dry air aloft will have increased significantly. Will \nkeep the mention of iso to sct showers/thunder in the forecast \nat this time. \n\nTuesday may see further influence of the closed low, with a\nreturn of moisture producing a continuation of cloud cover with\nthe chance of showers. These showers take advantage of daytime\nheating, likely more diurnally driven than associated with a\nfront. They may also be more widespread than the current\nforecast carries, but will see how timing and influence of the\nclosed low trends. The low finally loses its grasp on New\nEngland come midweek as it nudges northeast into the Canadian\nMaritimes. This would provide a break from the dreary conditions\nas temperatures remain seasonable.",
